hi everyone and welcome to biology  professor today I'm going to take you  through the history of humoral miasma  and German theories so basically these  are three different theories they're  three different ways of understanding  the spread of disease let's start with a  humoral theory  so it was dominant from ancient times to  the 1800s especially in the Western  world so when you think like medieval  times in Europe or the early American  colonies humoral theory is really how  people thought disease spread they  thought that disease was caused by  imbalances and foreign different bodily  liquids these were blood phlegm black  bile and yellow bile and each of these  liquids is associated with with  different temperaments with different  seasons but ultimately it was thought  that to have good health they had to be  balanced in a particular way and so  there were treatments that were aimed at  restoring the balance of these four  humors treatments like bloodletting with  leeches the use of laxatives also a  medics to make someone vomit blistering  of the skin or the throat things that we  know today are usually not helpful in  treating disease and they were just  trying to restore the balance here and  unfortunately often did really more harm  than good  now the next theory we're going to talk  about today is miasma theory it has also  been dominant in various places from  ancient  times two about the 1880s and so even  when the Western world began to give up  humoral theory they were then  transitioning to believing more in  miasma theory miasma theory held that  disease was caused by something called  miasma which was basically bad air sort  of filthy stinky gross air given off by  rotting organic matter and you can kind  of see how this made sense if there's a  lot of rotting dead bodies dead mice and  the road a lot of sort of human wastes  and feces that make things smell really  bad a lot of unwashed bodies cramps  together in cities that stink those are  all situations where there's a lot of  disease spread as well of course we now  know today that it's not just bad air  it's not just that smelly air makes  everybody say but this is really what  people believed in through the mid  eighteen hundreds in the late 1800s and  it had it sort of upside and that it led  to improvements in sewage and general  sanitation in major cities for example  one year what's called the year of the  great stink London smelled so bad the  Parliament couldn't even meet because  the air just smells too foul and so  because they also thought that that  horrible smell caused disease that led  to a lot of sort of sewage and  sanitation improvements in cities which  was a really good thing now let's move  on to talking about German theory  so germ theory is basically the modern  understanding of disease it gained wide  acceptance in the 1900s  after miasma theory was debunked  although as we'll talk about in a minute  there was early evidence that predated  the 1900s that was still in support of  germ theory germ theory says that  disease is caused by microorganisms or  microbes hence the name germ we have  about germs are talking about microbes  now there's a couple of caveats to keep  in mind one is that this is talking  about infectious disease only so  diseases that are caused by by bacteria  by fungi viruses we're not talking about  diseases caused by more genetic and  environmental components so things like  genetic disorders or cancers or or  diseases from exposure to pollutions or  toxins we're not talking about those  types of disease we're talking about  infectious disease and so infectious  disease is caused by microorganisms so  this is the modern understanding of  disease it's not caused by imbalances of  humor it's not caused by smell the air  is caused by microbes now this they'd  have to be updated so in the 1930s when  viruses were first being understood  viruses are not technically organisms  are not alive they're not considered  micro organisms and so the germ theory  had to be updated to be infectious  disease caused by microorganisms and  viruses it had to be updated again when  prions were discovered prions being  infectious proteins I know let's talk  about some of the early evidence for  germ theory as early as the late 1600s  we had a scientist Anton van Leeuwenhoek  who developed an early microscope and he  was the first person to actually see  bacteria under a microscope  he called them animal kill a or animal  cules which her face is basically sort  of latinum for tiny animals in the 1850s  we saw some public health efforts from  people who thought you know this miasma  theory it doesn't make sense to me  so people like Ignaz Semmelweis who said  no  then fever it's killing all these women  who have just given birth it's not  caused by bad air  I think it's caused by physicians not  washing their hands so he made people  start washing their hands and they were  delivering babies and sort of  post-delivery  infections went way down around the same  time John snow was looking at the  cholera outbreak in London and London  health officials said it's definitely  bad air and Johnson said that doesn't  make sense  I think it's dirty water and he actually  was able to trace it to the Broad Street  pump and prove that cholera was a  waterborne disease instead of an  airborne one in the late 1800s Pasteur  disproved spontaneous generation this  was the idea that living things could  come from nonliving matter he said no  they can't there's these microorganisms  that we can't see and then around the  same time Robert cook was developing  what are now known as cooks postulates  which are the set of rules we have to go  through and satisfy in order to say that  yes this disease is caused by this  organism and so all of these sort of  advances towards germ theory really  predated it's its final acceptance in  the 1900 so that's it today if you're  interested in these topics about  epidemiology and disease spread please  see my playlist for those types of  videos you can learn more about Koch's  postulates you can learn more about  various types of diseases and the way  that they're transmitted vectors things  like that so check that out and thanks  for watching biology professor
 

